# Folder structure (strict)

Organize `src/` by responsibility. Always import via the `@/*` alias (maps to `src/*` in `tsconfig.json`) — never deep relative paths like `../../../lib/utils`.

## src/ layout

- `app/` — Next.js App Router routes, layouts, and pages only
- `components/`
  - `ui/` — generic, reusable primitives (shadcn-style), **kebab-case** files
  - `common/` — shared, domain-aware components used across features
  - `<feature>/` — components specific to one feature (e.g. `pricing/`, `hero/`)
- `sections/` — full-page compositions, one **PascalCase** folder per page
- `hooks/` — custom React hooks, one per file, `useX` naming
- `lib/` — utilities, integrations, and cross-cutting shared types
- `utils/` — small standalone helper functions
- `store/` — Redux Toolkit: slices, selectors, and types grouped by feature
- `providers/` — React context providers and app-level setup
- `constants/` — app-wide constants
- `data/` — static fixtures / mock data
- `styles/` — global CSS

## Page composition (routes → sections)

Keep `app/` routes thin. A `page.tsx` should **only** render its top-level page component from
`sections/` — no markup or logic of its own. Each page gets a **PascalCase** folder in `sections/`
containing one main component (named after the folder) plus the section components it composes.

```
src/
├── app/
│   └── page.tsx              # only: import + render <HomePage />
└── sections/
    └── HomePage/
        ├── HomePage.tsx      # main page component, composes the sections below
        ├── HeroSection.tsx
        ├── FeatureSection.tsx
        └── ...               # other sections for this page
```

```tsx
// src/app/page.tsx
import HomePage from "@/sections/HomePage/HomePage"

const Page = () => <HomePage />

export default Page
```

```tsx
// src/sections/HomePage/HomePage.tsx
import HeroSection from "./HeroSection"
import FeatureSection from "./FeatureSection"

const HomePage = () => (
  <main>
    <HeroSection />
    <FeatureSection />
  </main>
)

export default HomePage
```

- The main page component (`HomePage.tsx`) is the single component referenced by `page.tsx`.
- Break each page into focused `*Section.tsx` components (`HeroSection`, `FeatureSection`, …) that
  the main page component composes.
- Section components that are reused across pages belong in `components/common/`; section components
  specific to one page stay inside that page's `sections/<PageName>/` folder.

## Naming

| Kind | Convention | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| React components | PascalCase `.tsx` | `PricingCard.tsx` |
| UI primitives (`ui/`) | kebab-case | `button.tsx`, `scroll-area.tsx` |
| Hooks | `use` + PascalCase | `useCheckout.ts` |
| Utilities / helpers | camelCase | `formatPrice.ts` |
| Redux files | `<feature>.slice.ts`, `<feature>.selectors.ts`, `<feature>.types.ts` | `global.slice.ts` |
| Props types | `*Props` suffix | `type PricingCardProps = { ... }` |

## One component per file (strict)

Every `.tsx` file must contain **exactly one** React component. Never declare two or more components
in the same file.

- If a component is built from sub-components (cards, rows, list items, illustrations, chart pieces,
  etc.), extract **each** sub-component into its own `.tsx` file and import it.
- Group a section's sub-components in a subfolder beside it, e.g. `sections/landing/features/DashboardCard.tsx`.
- Non-component code is exempt and should live in non-`.tsx` files (or co-located): types, constants,
  static data, custom hooks, helper/utility functions, and `cva`/variant definitions.
- The only component in the file should be the one the file is named after.

```
// ❌ Bad — Features.tsx defines BentoCard, DashboardCard, AlertsCard, … all in one file
// ✅ Good
sections/landing/
├── Features.tsx              # one component: <Features /> (composes the cards)
└── features/
    ├── BentoCard.tsx         # one component
    ├── DashboardCard.tsx     # one component
    ├── AlertsCard.tsx        # one component
    └── featureData.ts        # non-component: helpers, types, static data
```

## Rules

- **One component per file** (see above) — this is non-negotiable.
- **Named exports** for reusable components/utilities; **default export** only for page-level routes and `sections/` compositions.
- Co-locate component-specific types inline near the component; put cross-cutting/shared types in `lib/`.
- Group new code into the directory that matches its responsibility — do not pile feature logic into `app/`.
